# cdn-embed

The "literal embed" story: drop one HTML file on a static host and it
works. No bundler, no framework, no install.

## Running it

### Once the package is published to npm

Open `index.html` in a browser. It loads the engine from
[esm.sh](https://esm.sh) via an import map:

```html
<script type="importmap">
  { "imports": { "works-calendar-engine": "https://esm.sh/works-calendar-engine@0.1.2" } }
</script>
<script type="module">
  import { evaluateConflicts } from 'works-calendar-engine';
</script>
```

esm.sh resolves `date-fns` automatically — you don't need to declare it.

### Before the package is published (local testing)

`file://` URLs don't load ES modules reliably across browsers. Serve the
example directory with any static server, e.g. from the repo root:

```bash
npm run build          # produces dist/
npx serve .            # then open http://localhost:3000/examples/cdn-embed/
```

To point the import map at your local build instead of esm.sh, replace
the `imports` entry with a relative path:

```html
<script type="importmap">
  { "imports": { "works-calendar-engine": "/dist/index.js" } }
</script>
```

Note that the local-build path won't resolve `date-fns` for you — you'd
need an additional `imports` entry pointing at an esm.sh copy of
date-fns, or use a small bundle step. The esm.sh route is simpler for a
demo; the local route is what you'd want when iterating on the engine
itself.

## What this proves

- The engine ships as a real ES module — `<script type="module">`
  with no transform pipeline can consume it.
- The published surface includes the symbols a CDN consumer needs
  (`evaluateConflicts`, conflict rule types).
- The package has no DOM access, no `eval`, no remote calls. The same
  HTML works inside an `<iframe sandbox="allow-scripts">` or behind a
  CSP without `unsafe-eval` / `unsafe-inline`.

## Embedding into your own page

Copy the `<script type="importmap">` block and the `<script type="module">`
block. The UI bits (the `<ul>`, the buttons, the inline CSS) are demo
furniture — replace with your own.
